## Portainer's SELinux domain (SPEC.md §spec:portainer-service).
##
## Portainer holds the Docker control socket, which is root-equivalent access to
## the machine. Reaching that socket from inside a container needs an exemption
## from the machine's mandatory access control. This module is that exemption,
## and nothing more: the rest of the machine stays Enforcing.
##
## The exemption itself is two permissions: writing the socket file, and
## connecting to the daemon behind it. Both come from container_stream_connect(),
## which container-selinux maintains for exactly this purpose - the rules are not
## written out here, so they stay correct as upstream changes them.
##
## Everything else this module grants is not an exemption at all. It is the
## ordinary access every container on the machine already has, which a domain
## built from the template does not inherit - see the container_file_t block.
##
## Why a domain of its own rather than granting container_t: container_t is every
## container on the machine. Granting it there would hand the Docker socket to
## anything the operator ever deploys through Portainer, which is broader than
## running Portainer with full privileges - the thing §spec:portainer-service
## rejected. kantainer-portainer.service opts this one container in with
## --security-opt label=type:kantainer_portainer_t; nothing else can.

policy_module(kantainer_portainer, 1.0.0)

gen_require(`
	attribute container_domain;
	attribute container_net_domain;
	attribute container_file_type;
	type container_runtime_t;
	type container_file_t;
')

# Portainer's persistent data under /var/lib/portainer, and the staged
# administrator password. Its own type, so no other container can read either.
type kantainer_portainer_file_t;
container_file(kantainer_portainer_file_t)

container_domain_template(kantainer_portainer, kantainer_portainer)

# Portainer serves HTTPS on 9443, and container_domain alone cannot listen on a
# socket or bind a port - container-selinux keeps network access on a separate
# attribute, and every upstream domain that talks to the network takes it right
# after the template call. Without this the unit starts, fails to bind, and
# systemd gives up: the machine boots serving nothing, but only on an enforcing
# kernel, which is exactly where a build-time test cannot see it.
typeattribute kantainer_portainer_t container_net_domain;

# THE ACCESS EVERY CONTAINER HAS, WHICH THIS DOMAIN DOES NOT INHERIT.
#
# container_domain_template(kantainer_portainer, kantainer_portainer) expands to
# container_manage_files_template over kantainer_portainer_file_t - THIS domain's
# own type. That covers /var/lib/portainer and the staged password and nothing
# else, because the files inside the container are not labelled with it: both the
# Portainer binary in the image's layers and the container's writable upper layer
# are container_file_t.
#
# container_t has this access only because its own call is
# container_domain_template(container, container), where the file prefix happens
# to name container_file_t. Ours names our own type, so the same access has to be
# asked for here.
#
# WHAT THE MISSING `map` COSTS, because nothing on the machine says it. container-
# selinux grants container_domain `entrypoint` on container_file_t, so execve
# succeeds - and then the loader's mmap of the binary is denied. Portainer dies
# from SIGSEGV before it can log a word: systemd reports status=139, the journal
# carries no Portainer output at all, and the only evidence anywhere on the
# machine is the AVC.
#
# NOT container_manage_files_template(kantainer_portainer, container), which is
# the tidier way to say this: that template also emits an fs_tmpfs_filetrans, and
# a second one conflicts with the type_transition the first call already made.
#
# This widens nothing. MCS isolates this container from every other one - the
# runtime gives each container its own categories, and only the shared image
# layers carry none - so what is granted here is what any container already has.
manage_dirs_pattern(kantainer_portainer_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
manage_files_pattern(kantainer_portainer_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
manage_lnk_files_pattern(kantainer_portainer_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
exec_files_pattern(kantainer_portainer_t, container_file_t, container_file_t)
allow kantainer_portainer_t container_file_t:dir_file_class_set map;

# Docker starts containers with NoNewPrivileges, which needs this transition.
container_runtime_typebounds(kantainer_portainer_t)

# The exemption itself.
container_stream_connect(kantainer_portainer_t)
